{"code":"def solve(parameters):
    if not isinstance(parameters, dict):
        raise ValueError('parameters must be a dict')
    if set(parameters.keys()) != {'operands', 'operators'}:
        raise ValueError('unexpected keys')
    operands = parameters['operands']
    operators = parameters['operators']
    if not isinstance(operands, list) or not isinstance(operators, list):
        raise ValueError('operands and operators must be lists')
    n = len(operands)
    if n < 1 or n > 8:
        raise ValueError('operand count out of range')
    if len(operators) != n - 1:
        raise ValueError('operator count must be len(operands)-1')
    for x in operands:
        if isinstance(x, bool) or not isinstance(x, int):
            raise ValueError('operands must be integers')
        if x < -10 or x > 20:
            raise ValueError('operand out of range')
    for op in operators:
        if op not in ('+', '-', '*'):
            raise ValueError('invalid operator')
    if n == 1:
        return str(1)
    dp = [[None] * n for _ in range(n)]
    for i in range(n):
        dp[i][i] = {operands[i]}
    for length in range(2, n + 1):
        for i in range(0, n - length + 1):
            j = i + length - 1
            vals = set()
            for k in range(i, j):
                op = operators[k]
                left = dp[i][k]
                right = dp[k + 1][j]
                for x in left:
                    for y in right:
                        if op == '+':
                            vals.add(x + y)
                        elif op == '-':
                            vals.add(x - y)
                        else:
                            vals.add(x * y)
            dp[i][j] = vals
    return str(len(dp[0][n - 1]))
","description":"Interval dynamic programming over sets of achievable integer values. For each contiguous subexpression a[i..j], dp[i][j] is the set of all values obtainable by fully parenthesizing it. Base case dp[i][i]={a[i]}. For longer intervals, every split point k combines each value from the left interval with each value from the right interval using the operator at position k, inserting results into a set to deduplicate. The answer is the cardinality of dp[0][n-1]. Inputs are validated against the schema (operand count 1..8, integer operands in [-10,20], operators in {+,-,*}, exactly n-1 operators); malformed inputs raise ValueError. The computation is exact and deterministic. Uncertainty: worst-case value sets can grow quickly, but the bounded input size keeps it feasible; no semantic ambiguity remains.","applicability":"Applies to expressions given as an ordered list of integer operands and a list of binary operators (+,-,*) with exactly len(operands)-1 operators, where parentheses may be inserted freely without reordering operands or operators. Returns the number of distinct integer values over all full parenthesizations. Limited to the schema bounds (1 to 8 operands, operands in [-10,20]); does not handle other operators, non-integer operands, or reordering."}
